1. What is false sharing?

False sharing occurs when multiple threads modify different variables located in the same cache line. The CPU cache coherence protocol repeatedly 
invalidates and transfers the cache line between cores, causing unnecessary performance over.

2. How do you prevent false sharing?

Common techniques include:

Using alignas(64) to align frequently modified variables.
Adding padding so each thread's data occupies a separate cache line.
Designing data structures so each thread writes
Is false sharing a correctness issue?

No. False sharing does not cause incorrect results. It is a performance issue. 
The program remains correct, but it may scale poorly because of excessive cache coherence traffic.


#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

struct Counter
{
    int x;
    int y;
};

Counter counter;

void thread1()
{
    for(int i=0;i<100000000;i++)
        counter.x++;
}

void thread2()
{
    for(int i=0;i<100000000;i++)
        counter.y++;
}

int main()
{
    std::thread t1(thread1);
    std::thread t2(thread2);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
}

Modern C++ provides a cleaner solution.

#include <new>

struct Counter
{
    alignas(64) int x;
    alignas(64) int y;
};
